% IMPORTANT (baposter mechanics, learned the hard way):
%  1) bottomaligned=X needs box X to already be typeset earlier IN THE
%     SOURCE (pgf resolves that anchor in source order, not by column/
%     visual position). So in every row below, the "reference" box (the
%     one with height=auto) is written FIRST, and the box(es) using
%     bottomaligned=<reference> are written right after it.
%  2) below=auto does NOT mean "flush under the header" -- it means
%     "below whatever box was written immediately before me in the
%     source" (baposter.cls tracks a single \@@previousbox, ignoring
%     columns). A box left with NO "below"/"aligned" key defaults to
%     row=0, which IS flush under the header -- that is what every
%     first box of Row 1 uses; only cross-row references use an
%     explicit below=<name>.
%  3) bottomaligned only overrides the box's FRAME height -- it does
%     NOT reflow or shrink the box's content. If the dependent box's
%     own content is taller than the reference box, that content
%     overflows past its own border. So the reference box in each row
%     must be the one that naturally needs the most room (Row 1:
%     "setup", the circuit figure + parameter list; Row 2: "histograms",
%     the 2x2 grid; Row 3: "dynamics", the wide time series). If you
%     grow the dependent box's content (abstractintro / asymmetry /
%     survival) it is safe to make it SHORTER than the reference (blank
%     space) but NOT taller (silent overflow) -- recompile and check.
%  4) Row 4 uses a different, more robust trick: all three boxes
%     (conclusions, references, colorlegend) use below=dynamics AND
%     above=bottom together. "above=bottom" pins the box's bottom edge
%     to the physical page bottom regardless of content, so all three
%     automatically share the exact same top (dynamics' bottom) and
%     bottom (page bottom) -- i.e. the same height -- with no
%     bottomaligned/ordering dependency between them at all. The only
%     requirement is that "conclusions" (deliberately the longest of
%     the three) still fits in that fixed height; if you lengthen it,
%     recompile and confirm nothing is clipped at the very bottom.
%  5) baposter never reflows content to fit the physical page: if the
%     sum of all row heights exceeds the A0 page, the excess is simply
%     clipped, invisibly, with no error and no second page. Every large
%     image below sits inside a minipage narrower than the box (not the
%     raw box width), so width=\linewidth (rule: always use it) still
%     holds while leaving headroom for Row 4.
