		\begin{posterbox}[name=setup,column=3,height=auto,span=3]{{Experimental Setup}}
			\small
			The FSR strip is glued along the full inner perimeter of the circular arena, so it integrates the total force the swarm exerts on the wall into a single conductance $G=1/R_{FSR}$.
			\vspace{0.05em}
			\begin{center}
				% --- CHANGE CIRCUIT WIDTH HERE (always scaled to \linewidth, never overflows) ---
				\resizebox{0.9\linewidth}{!}{%
					\begin{circuitikz}[american voltages, scale=1.2, every node/.append style={font=\LARGE}, color=sageDark]
						\node[op amp] (opamp) at (3.4,0) {};
						\draw (opamp.-) -- ++(-0.7,0) coordinate(sj)
						to[vR, l=$R_{FSR}$, *-*] ++(-2.0,0) coordinate(fsrleft);
						\draw (fsrleft) -- ++(0,-0.35) coordinate(battop);
						\draw (battop) to[battery1, l_={$V_{exc}$}] ++(0,-1.15) node[ground]{};
						\draw (opamp.+) -- ++(-0.5,0) -- ++(0,-0.7) node[ground]{};
						\coordinate (fbtop) at ($(opamp.out)+(0,1.1)$);
						\coordinate (fbleft) at (sj |- fbtop);
						\draw (sj) -- (fbleft);
						\draw (fbleft) to[R, l={$R_f$}] (fbtop);
						\draw (fbtop) -- (opamp.out);
						\node[draw=sageDark, thick, fill=sagePale, minimum width=2.6cm, minimum height=2.0cm, inner sep=5pt, align=center]
						(mcu) at ($(opamp.out)+(2.1,0)$) {\Large\textbf{MCU}\\[3pt] \large 10-bit ADC\\ Averaging ($N{=}10$)};
						\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (opamp.out) -- (mcu.west);
						\draw[-{Latex[length=2.2mm]}, thick] (mcu.east) -- ++(1.0,0) node[right, align=left] {\textbf{UART}\\[-1pt] 115200 bd};
						\fill (sj) circle(1.6pt);
					\end{circuitikz}%
				}
			\end{center}
			\vspace{0.1em}
			An inverting transimpedance stage converts $R_{FSR}$ into a voltage $V_{\text{out}}=-V_{exc}\,R_f/R_{FSR}$, which the MCU digitizes and streams over UART; the excitation is fixed and inverted so the divider sits in its most sensitive range. With $R_f=10\,\mathrm{k}\Omega$, $G\,[\mu\text{S}]=1/R_{FSR}\propto \text{ADC}_{\text{raw}}$, and averaging $N=10$ raw samples on-board suppresses high-frequency electrical noise before each reading is logged.
			\vspace{0.2em}
			\begin{center}
				\fcolorbox{scopeBorder}{scopeBg}{\parbox{0.7\linewidth}{%
						\footnotesize\textbf{Parameters}\\[0.25em]
						\begin{itemize}[itemsep=1.5pt,topsep=1pt,leftmargin=1.0em]
							\item $N=\{0,25,26,27\}$
							\item Frame rate: $20\,\mathrm{Hz}$
							\item Duration: $30\,\mathrm{min/run}$
							\item Excitation: $-0.7534\,\mathrm{V}$
							\item Baud rate: $115200\,\mathrm{bps}$
						\end{itemize}%
				}}
			\end{center}
		\end{posterbox}
		
		\begin{posterbox}[name=abstractintro,column=0,bottomaligned=setup,span=3]{{Abstract \& Introduction}}
\small
% --- Abstract: keep this to ONE line (widen/shorten it if you edit) ---
\textbf{Abstract.} A low-cost FSR-strip sensor for direct, continuous wall-pressure readout in confined Kilobot swarms.

\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{Introduction.} Pressure in active matter is generally \textbf{not a state function}: it depends on the container's shape, not only on density and activity. Prior studies infer it only \textbf{indirectly}, from wall deflection or a single-point load cell; our FSR strip instead reads the wall force directly and continuously.
\vspace{0.4em}

\fcolorbox{scopeBorder}{scopeBg}{\parbox{0.6\linewidth}{\footnotesize\vspace{0.1em}\textbf{Scope:} results here are for the \textbf{integral} configuration only; the distributed multi-strip sensor is future work.\vspace{0.1em}}}
\end{posterbox}
		
