		\begin{posterbox}[name=conclusions,column=0,below=dynamics,above=bottom,span=3]{{Conclusions}}
			\small
			We built a low-cost FSR-strip sensor that reads the pressure of a confined Kilobot swarm directly and continuously, without wall deflection or point load cells. Increasing colony density does not simply rescale the pressure signal: it widens the fluctuation distribution, increases its skewness, and thickens the survival tail, all tied to the onset and collapse of jamming arches -- consistent with active-matter pressure not being captured by density and activity alone. The distributed multi-strip array, already built, is the natural next step: localizing these forces in space and testing a geometry-aware equation of state.
		\end{posterbox}
		
